(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-physiology-master-exam-version-8"]={"title":"Physiology Master Exam — Form D (70 Questions)","category":"Physiology","exam":"Exam 3","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Plasma differs from serum in that plasma still contains...","choices":["antibodies that are absent from serum as it typically occurs here","dissolved oxygen that serum cannot hold","far more red blood cells than serum does","the clotting proteins such as fibrinogen that serum has lost"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Plasma is the fluid obtained when clotting is prevented, so it retains fibrinogen and the other clotting factors; serum is the fluid left after blood has clotted, meaning those clotting proteins have been consumed and removed. That is the defining difference between the two. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 3-5"},{"q":"Platelets (thrombocytes) are best described as...","choices":["cell fragments pinched off from large megakaryocytes in the marrow","oxygen-carrying cells closely related to red blood cells in most cases","a specialized type of white blood cell that fights infection","mature nucleated cells that divide within the bloodstream"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Platelets are not whole cells but membrane-bound fragments shed from megakaryocytes in the bone marrow; despite lacking a nucleus, they carry granules and machinery essential for hemostasis. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 6, 30-33"},{"q":"Anemia is fundamentally a state in which...","choices":["the blood's oxygen-carrying capacity is reduced below normal","the white blood cell count falls below the normal range","arterial oxygen partial pressure is always markedly low","the blood carries too much oxygen for the tissues to use"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Anemia means a deficiency of functional hemoglobin (from too few red cells or too little hemoglobin per cell), which lowers the amount of oxygen the blood can carry and deliver to tissues, regardless of the specific cause. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 20-21"},{"q":"In polycythemia the oxygen-carrying capacity rises, yet the condition can impair circulation because...","choices":["the plasma volume expands and dilutes the hemoglobin","the kidneys stop producing erythropoietin entirely","the extra red cells cannot bind any oxygen at all as it typically occurs here","the accompanying rise in blood viscosity increases resistance to flow"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Although more red cells raise oxygen-carrying capacity, the higher hematocrit sharply increases blood viscosity; the thicker blood resists flow and raises the load on the heart, which can offset or outweigh the benefit of extra oxygen capacity. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 6"},{"q":"The normal circulating lifespan of a red blood cell is about...","choices":["several years, being among the longest-lived cells","120 days, after which macrophages remove the aged cell","30 days, limited by the loss of their mitochondria","a few hours, similar to the lifespan of a neutrophil"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Red cells survive roughly 120 days; lacking a nucleus and organelles, they cannot repair themselves and become fragile, so macrophages (mainly in the spleen and liver) phagocytize the senescent cells and recycle their components. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 14-17"},{"q":"Vitamin B12 and folate are needed for red cell production chiefly because they...","choices":["form the globin chains that make up the hemoglobin molecule","support DNA synthesis required for the rapid division of precursor cells","stimulate the kidney to release more erythropoietin under most normal conditions","provide the iron atom that sits at the center of each heme"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Erythropoiesis involves rapid cell division, which demands DNA synthesis; B12 and folate are cofactors for that process. Their deficiency impairs nuclear maturation, producing large, poorly functional cells (megaloblastic anemia). — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slide 17"},{"q":"The extrinsic pathway of coagulation is triggered when...","choices":["plasmin begins to break down an existing fibrin clot during this process","blood contacts negatively charged collagen within the vessel","platelets release erythropoietin at the site of injury","tissue factor from damaged cells outside the vessel contacts the blood"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The extrinsic (tissue factor) pathway begins when trauma exposes blood to tissue factor released by damaged extravascular cells; this rapidly activates the cascade toward prothrombin activator, and it is the faster of the two initiating pathways. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 44-46"},{"q":"Vitamin K is essential for coagulation because it...","choices":["binds calcium so it cannot participate in the cascade","directly dissolves clots once a wound has healed during this particular process in the tissues","is the enzyme that converts fibrinogen into fibrin","is required by the liver to synthesize several clotting factors, including prothrombin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Vitamin K is a cofactor the liver uses to produce functional prothrombin and factors VII, IX, and X; without it, these factors are made in an inactive form, and clotting is impaired — the basis of warfarin's action. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 46-47"},{"q":"Von Willebrand factor contributes to hemostasis mainly by...","choices":["stimulating the marrow to produce more platelets under most normal conditions","converting prothrombin directly into active thrombin","helping platelets adhere to the exposed vessel wall at the injury site","dissolving the fibrin meshwork after the wound heals"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Von Willebrand factor bridges platelet surface receptors to exposed subendothelial collagen, anchoring platelets at the site of injury; it also stabilizes factor VIII in the circulation, so its deficiency causes a bleeding tendency. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 33-34"},{"q":"Intact, undamaged endothelium helps prevent unwanted clotting by...","choices":["actively cross-linking any fibrin that forms nearby during this process in the tissues","presenting a smooth, negatively charged surface that repels platelets and factors","absorbing all calcium out of the surrounding plasma","continuously releasing thrombin into the passing blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Healthy endothelium is smooth and carries a negative surface charge that repels platelets and clotting factors, and it secretes antithrombotic substances; this keeps blood fluid until injury exposes the underlying tissue and triggers hemostasis. — Hemostasis & Coagulation, slides 34, 48"},{"q":"To reach an infected tissue, white blood cells first leave the capillary by...","choices":["squeezing between endothelial cells in a process called diapedesis","dissolving the entire capillary wall with digestive enzymes","converting into red blood cells that diffuse across the wall","being carried passively into the tissue by bulk fluid flow in the body"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Leukocytes exit the bloodstream by diapedesis — deforming and slipping between the endothelial cells of the capillary or venule wall — and then move toward the infection by chemotaxis, following chemical gradients released at the site. — Blood & Immunology, slides 26-28"},{"q":"Compared with neutrophils, macrophages are able to...","choices":["phagocytize many more particles and survive to keep functioning","divide rapidly within the blood to expand their numbers","carry oxygen to the site to support the inflammatory response","produce specific antibodies against the ingested organism"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Macrophages are larger, longer-lived, and far more powerful phagocytes than neutrophils; they can engulf many organisms and large debris and continue functioning, whereas neutrophils typically ingest a limited number and then die. — Blood & Immunology, slides 29-31"},{"q":"T-lymphocytes are so named because they mature in the...","choices":["thymus, where they are selected for function and self-tolerance","thyroid, under the direct control of thyroid hormone","tonsils, which serve as their primary maturation site","tibial marrow, a specialized region of long bones under these conditions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. T-cells complete their maturation in the thymus, where they undergo selection that keeps useful, self-tolerant clones and deletes strongly self-reactive ones; the 'T' reflects this thymic education. — Blood & Immunology, slides 40-42"},{"q":"B-lymphocytes complete their maturation in the...","choices":["liver, throughout the whole of adult life in the body","spleen, which is their only site of origin","thymus, alongside developing T-lymphocytes","bone marrow, before circulating to lymphoid tissues"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In mammals, B-cells mature in the bone marrow (the 'B' is often associated with the bursa in birds, but marrow in humans); once mature, they seed lymph nodes, spleen, and other lymphoid tissue where they can respond to antigen. — Blood & Immunology, slides 40-44"},{"q":"Opsonization enhances phagocytosis by...","choices":["attracting white cells toward the infection along a gradient","coating a microbe with antibody or complement so phagocytes grip it more easily","dissolving the microbe's cell wall before it is engulfed","forming memory cells for a faster response next time during this process in the tissues"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Opsonization tags a pathogen with antibody or complement fragments that phagocyte receptors recognize; this greatly increases the efficiency with which neutrophils and macrophages attach to and engulf the organism. — Blood & Immunology, slides 30, 53"},{"q":"Long-lasting immunity after an infection depends on...","choices":["a permanent rise in body temperature that deters the pathogen","antibodies from the first infection remaining in the blood forever","memory lymphocytes that persist and respond rapidly on re-exposure","neutrophils that stay elevated permanently after the infection"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. During a response, some activated B- and T-cells become long-lived memory cells; on later exposure to the same antigen they mount a faster, stronger secondary response, which is the basis of durable immunity and of vaccination. — Blood & Immunology, slides 45-48"},{"q":"Antibodies help neutralize threats directly by...","choices":["binding and inactivating toxins or blocking a microbe's ability to attach","synthesizing new complement proteins at the infection site","engulfing and digesting bacteria the way phagocytes do","forming pores that lyse the host's own infected cells in the body's tissues"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Beyond tagging pathogens for other defenses, antibodies can directly neutralize by binding toxins to render them harmless and by coating microbes to block the sites they use to attach to and enter host cells. — Blood & Immunology, slides 50-53"},{"q":"Which is an example of an innate (nonspecific) defense?","choices":["memory B-cells produced after a specific vaccination during this process","intact skin and acidic gastric secretions that block or kill microbes","clonal selection of a lymphocyte matching one antigen","high-affinity IgG antibodies tailored to one pathogen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Innate defenses are present from birth and act nonspecifically; physical and chemical barriers such as intact skin, mucus, cilia, and acidic stomach secretions prevent entry or destroy many microbes without needing prior exposure. — Blood & Immunology, slides 18-20, 60"},{"q":"In an immediate allergic reaction, symptoms arise when an allergen...","choices":["neutralizes complement so inflammation cannot occur","cross-links IgE on mast cells, triggering release of histamine","stimulates the marrow to overproduce red blood cells","is engulfed by cytotoxic T-cells that then release perforin"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In allergy, IgE bound to mast cells and basophils is cross-linked by the allergen, causing these cells to degranulate and release histamine and other mediators; the resulting vasodilation, increased permeability, and smooth-muscle effects produce allergic symptoms. — Blood & Immunology, slides 64-65"},{"q":"Self versus non-self recognition by T-cells relies on...","choices":["histamine tags that mark self cells as friendly under these conditions","antibodies coating every one of the body's own cells","the color of the surface pigments on each host cell","MHC molecules that display cell peptides for T-cell inspection"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Major histocompatibility complex (MHC) molecules on cell surfaces present peptide fragments; T-cells inspect these displays and react to foreign peptides while tolerating self, which is how the adaptive system distinguishes self from non-self. — Blood & Immunology, slides 55-58"},{"q":"Peristalsis differs from segmentation in that peristalsis...","choices":["propels contents forward along the tract, while segmentation mainly mixes them","is a voluntary movement, while segmentation is involuntary","occurs only in the esophagus and nowhere else in the gut during this particular process","only mixes contents, while segmentation moves them forward"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Peristalsis is a progressive wave of contraction behind the bolus that moves contents onward, whereas segmentation is alternating localized contractions that chop and mix contents with digestive juices without net forward movement. — GI Physiology, slides 40-46"},{"q":"Gastrin promotes digestion primarily by...","choices":["inhibiting all secretion during the intestinal phase","contracting the gallbladder to release stored bile","neutralizing stomach acid to protect the duodenum","stimulating gastric acid secretion and gastric motility"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Gastrin, released by antral G-cells in response to protein and stomach distension, stimulates parietal-cell acid secretion (largely via histamine release) and increases gastric motility, preparing the stomach to process a meal. — GI Physiology, slides 13-15, 96"},{"q":"Between meals, the migrating motor complex serves to...","choices":["trigger the release of bile in response to fat during this process","sweep residual contents and bacteria along the gut during fasting","mix a large meal thoroughly with digestive enzymes","absorb the bulk of dietary nutrients into the blood"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The migrating motor complex is a cyclic pattern of contractions during fasting (associated with motilin) that sweeps leftover food particles, secretions, and bacteria toward the colon — a 'housekeeping' function that keeps the small intestine clear between meals. — GI Physiology, slides 40-46"},{"q":"Salivary amylase begins the digestion of...","choices":["fat, by emulsifying it into small droplets","nucleic acids, releasing their component nucleotides","starch, though its action is soon halted by stomach acid","protein, which it splits into individual amino acids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Salivary amylase (ptyalin) starts breaking down starch in the mouth; when the bolus reaches the acidic stomach, the low pH inactivates the enzyme, and starch digestion resumes in the small intestine with pancreatic amylase. — GI Physiology, slides 76-79, 123"},{"q":"Bile salts emulsify fat effectively because each molecule...","choices":["chemically digests the triglyceride into fatty acids itself","binds oxygen to help oxidize the fat for energy in the body's tissues","is entirely water-soluble and dissolves the fat outright","has both a water-soluble and a fat-soluble end (it is amphipathic)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bile salts are amphipathic: one part associates with water and the other with lipid. This lets them coat fat droplets and break them into many tiny droplets, greatly increasing the surface area available for lipase to act on. — GI Physiology, slides 130-133"},{"q":"Pancreatic proteases such as trypsin are secreted as inactive precursors that are then activated...","choices":["only after the food has entered the large intestine as it typically occurs here","within the pancreatic cells immediately after synthesis","by stomach acid before they reach the small intestine","in the intestinal lumen, so the pancreas is not digested by its own enzymes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Proteases are released as zymogens (e.g., trypsinogen) and activated only after reaching the small intestine — enterokinase converts trypsinogen to trypsin, which then activates the others. This delay protects the pancreas from self-digestion. — GI Physiology, slides 101-105"},{"q":"Kupffer cells within the liver contribute to defense by...","choices":["producing bile pigments for excretion into the intestine","secreting insulin to regulate the blood glucose level","phagocytizing bacteria and debris carried in from the gut via portal blood","storing iron recovered from the diet for later use under most normal conditions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Kupffer cells are resident macrophages lining the liver sinusoids; because portal blood from the intestine passes through the liver, they are positioned to engulf bacteria and debris before that blood enters the general circulation. — Ch 71 Liver, slides 6-8"},{"q":"Water is absorbed across the intestinal wall mainly by...","choices":["binding to bile salts that carry it into the enterocyte","digestion of water molecules by brush-border enzymes during this process","osmosis, following the absorption of solutes such as sodium and glucose","active transport pumps that move water directly against gradients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Water absorption is passive: as sodium, glucose, amino acids, and other solutes are absorbed, they raise the osmotic pull on the blood side, and water follows by osmosis. This is why efficient solute uptake drives water uptake. — GI Physiology, slides 140-143"},{"q":"Adequate absorption of the fat-soluble vitamins A, D, E, and K depends on...","choices":["the presence of bile salts to incorporate them into micelles","conversion into water-soluble forms before uptake","a specialized active pump unique to each vitamin in this scenario","an acidic intestinal pH created by gastric secretions"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fat-soluble vitamins are absorbed along with dietary fat; bile salts are needed to form the micelles that ferry them to the enterocyte surface. Impaired bile flow therefore reduces uptake of these vitamins. — GI Physiology, slides 132-133, 144"},{"q":"The gastrocolic reflex helps explain why...","choices":["bile is released the moment food enters the mouth","nutrients are absorbed faster in the large intestine","the stomach empties immediately when the colon fills","eating a meal often stimulates the urge to defecate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The gastrocolic reflex links stomach filling to increased colonic motility: distension of the stomach triggers mass movements in the colon that propel contents toward the rectum, which is why a meal frequently prompts the urge to defecate. — GI Physiology, slides 59-66"},{"q":"The liver protects the body from many ingested and internally produced chemicals by...","choices":["secreting toxins unchanged directly into the bloodstream","storing all toxins permanently within its own cells in this scenario","metabolizing drugs and toxins into forms that can be excreted","converting toxins into oxygen for the tissues to use"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Hepatocytes carry out biotransformation, chemically modifying drugs, hormones, and toxins (often making them more water-soluble) so they can be excreted in bile or urine; this detoxifying role is a central hepatic function. — Ch 71 Liver, slides 8-10"},{"q":"The liver disposes of the nitrogen from amino-acid breakdown by...","choices":["converting the ammonia into glucose for energy under these conditions","storing the ammonia in hepatocytes until it is needed","exhaling the ammonia directly through the lungs","converting toxic ammonia into urea for excretion by the kidneys"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Deamination of amino acids releases ammonia, which is highly toxic; the liver's urea cycle converts it into urea, a far less toxic compound that the kidneys excrete. Liver failure allows ammonia to accumulate. — Ch 70, slides 16-17; Ch 71, slide 10"},{"q":"Glycolysis in the cytoplasm yields, per glucose molecule, a net of...","choices":["36 ATP and carbon dioxide, requiring oxygen throughout","2 ATP and 2 pyruvate, even in the absence of oxygen","no ATP at all, serving only to store glucose as glycogen","2 ATP and lactate, which can occur only with oxygen present"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Glycolysis produces a net of 2 ATP (and 2 NADH) and 2 pyruvate molecules per glucose, and it proceeds without oxygen; this modest yield is why anaerobic tissues rely on it but generate far less ATP than aerobic metabolism. — Ch 68, slides 10-16"},{"q":"Most of the ATP from complete glucose oxidation is generated during...","choices":["fermentation of pyruvate to lactate in the cytoplasm","oxidative phosphorylation on the inner mitochondrial membrane","the initial phosphorylation of glucose to glucose-6-phosphate","glycolysis, which produces the bulk of the cell's ATP"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. While glycolysis and the citric acid cycle yield some ATP, the great majority is produced by oxidative phosphorylation, where electrons from NADH and FADH2 drive the electron transport chain and ATP synthase; this stage requires oxygen. — Ch 68, slides 16-22"},{"q":"Glucagon and insulin have opposing effects on blood glucose in that glucagon...","choices":["has no effect on blood glucose, unlike insulin","and insulin both raise blood glucose together","lowers blood glucose while insulin raises it","raises blood glucose while insulin lowers it"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Glucagon (from pancreatic alpha cells) raises blood glucose by promoting hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis, whereas insulin (from beta cells) lowers it by promoting cellular uptake and storage; together they keep glucose within a narrow range. — Ch 68, slides 4, 30; Ch 71, slide 9"},{"q":"Fatty acids are broken down for energy through beta-oxidation, which...","choices":["converts fatty acids directly into glucose for the brain","builds fatty acids up from acetyl-CoA for storage in the body's tissues","removes two-carbon units to form acetyl-CoA for the citric acid cycle","splits fatty acids into individual carbon atoms in the cytoplasm"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Beta-oxidation sequentially cleaves two-carbon acetyl-CoA units from fatty acids (in the mitochondria); the acetyl-CoA enters the citric acid cycle, and the process also yields NADH and FADH2, making fat a rich energy source. — Ch 69, slides 10-14"},{"q":"During prolonged fasting, the liver produces ketone bodies because...","choices":["ketones are the only fuel the heart can ever use during this particular process in the tissues","the body has an excess of glucose that must be stored","fatty acid breakdown outpaces the cycle's capacity, and ketones offer an alternative fuel","insulin levels are high and drive ketone formation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In fasting, abundant acetyl-CoA from fatty-acid oxidation exceeds what the citric acid cycle can process (partly because oxaloacetate is diverted to gluconeogenesis); the liver converts the surplus into ketone bodies, which tissues like the brain can use as fuel. — Ch 69, slides 15-19"},{"q":"High-density lipoprotein (HDL) is often called 'good' cholesterol because it...","choices":["prevents the liver from making any cholesterol at all in the body's tissues","delivers cholesterol from the liver to the artery walls","is the main particle that carries dietary fat from the gut","carries excess cholesterol from tissues back to the liver for disposal"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. HDL performs reverse cholesterol transport, picking up excess cholesterol from peripheral tissues and returning it to the liver for excretion in bile; this helps clear cholesterol from vessel walls, in contrast to LDL, which delivers cholesterol to tissues. — Ch 69, slides 6, 36"},{"q":"Before an amino acid's carbon skeleton can be used for energy, its amino group is removed by...","choices":["beta-oxidation, which removes nitrogen two atoms at a time","glycolysis, which strips the nitrogen as it makes ATP","deamination or transamination, freeing the skeleton for metabolism","the electron transport chain during ATP synthesis under these conditions"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Removing the amino group by deamination (releasing ammonia) or transamination (transferring it to a keto acid) frees the carbon skeleton to enter energy pathways as a glucogenic or ketogenic intermediate, while the nitrogen is routed to urea. — Ch 70, slides 14-17"},{"q":"Body protein is used for energy only as a later resort because...","choices":["protein contains far more energy per gram than fat does under most conditions in the body","protein serves essential structural and functional roles the body preserves first","the body cannot break protein down under any conditions","protein is stored in large depots specifically for fuel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Proteins are needed for enzymes, structure, transport, and immunity, so the body preferentially burns carbohydrate and fat and spares protein; only in prolonged starvation, once fat reserves are heavily depleted, does significant tissue-protein breakdown for fuel occur. — Ch 70, slides 3-6; Ch 72, slide 18"},{"q":"Per gram, the energy yields of the macronutrients are approximately...","choices":["equal energy from all three, at about 4 kcal per gram","9 kcal for carbohydrate, 9 for protein, and 4 for fat","4 kcal for carbohydrate, 4 for protein, and 9 for fat","4 kcal for fat, with carbohydrate and protein at 9 kcal"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Carbohydrate and protein each provide about 4 kcal per gram, while fat provides about 9 kcal per gram; fat's much higher energy density is why it is the body's most compact form of energy storage. — Ch 72, slides 4-5"},{"q":"Obesity develops when, over time,...","choices":["energy intake persistently exceeds energy expenditure","the body loses its ability to store any fat at all","dietary protein is completely eliminated from the diet","energy expenditure persistently exceeds energy intake"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Obesity is fundamentally a chronic positive energy balance: when caloric intake exceeds expenditure over an extended period, the surplus is stored as body fat. Genetic, hormonal, and behavioral factors influence intake and expenditure, but the balance principle underlies it. — Ch 72, slides 2-3, 16"},{"q":"During early starvation, before fat stores are heavily used, the body first relies on...","choices":["stored glycogen, which is exhausted within roughly a day","ketone bodies, which are the very first fuel mobilized","body protein, which is broken down before any glycogen","dietary intake, which continues to supply energy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The body first draws on liver (and muscle) glycogen for glucose, but these stores are small and are largely depleted within about 24 hours; the body then shifts increasingly to fat oxidation and, later, ketone production and protein breakdown. — Ch 72, slides 17-19"},{"q":"The hypothalamus acts as the body's thermostat, with the responses to cold coordinated so that the body...","choices":["raises its set point to induce a protective fever","loses heat through vasodilation and sweating during this process in the tissues","stops all metabolic heat production to save energy","conserves and generates heat through vasoconstriction and shivering"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When cold is sensed, the hypothalamus triggers heat-conserving and heat-generating responses: cutaneous vasoconstriction to reduce loss and shivering (plus increased metabolism) to produce heat, defending core temperature. — Ch 74, slides 4, 11-15"},{"q":"Heat loss by conduction and convection differ in that convection...","choices":["emits heat as infrared waves without any medium in this scenario","carries heat away by the movement of air or fluid over the skin","transfers heat only to objects touching the skin directly","requires evaporation of sweat to remove any heat"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Conduction transfers heat to objects or air in direct contact, while convection removes heat as moving air or water carries the warmed layer away and replaces it with cooler medium; a breeze increases convective loss. — Ch 74, slides 6-9"},{"q":"With repeated exposure to a hot environment, acclimatization improves heat tolerance largely by...","choices":["increasing the sweating rate and conserving salt in the sweat","shutting down cutaneous blood flow to the skin as a general rule","reducing the sweating rate to conserve body water","lowering the hypothalamic set point permanently"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Heat acclimatization enhances the capacity to sweat earlier and more profusely (improving evaporative cooling) and, via aldosterone, conserves sodium in the sweat, protecting the body's salt balance during heavy sweating. — Ch 74, slides 8-10"},{"q":"Larger individuals tend to have a higher total basal metabolic rate partly because...","choices":["surface area has no relationship to heat loss or metabolism","larger bodies have proportionally less active tissue during this process","a bigger body always has a much lower body temperature","a greater body mass and surface area require more energy to maintain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Basal metabolic rate scales with body size: more metabolically active tissue and a larger surface area (over which heat is lost) mean more total energy is needed to maintain the resting body, which is why BMR is often expressed relative to body size. — Ch 73, slides 8-11"},{"q":"When a fever finally 'breaks,' the person often flushes and sweats because the hypothalamic set point has...","choices":["returned to normal, so the now-high body temperature is shed by sweating and vasodilation","risen further, causing the body to generate even more heat","been abolished, leaving temperature completely unregulated","dropped far below normal, triggering intense shivering during this particular process in the tissues"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. When pyrogen levels fall, the set point drops back to normal; the body temperature is now above the set point, so the hypothalamus activates heat-loss responses — vasodilation and sweating — to bring temperature down, producing the flush and sweat of the crisis. — Ch 74, slides 18-20"},{"q":"Basal metabolic rate tends to decline with advancing age largely because of...","choices":["a steady rise in the amount of active muscle tissue","a permanent increase in body temperature over time","a gradual loss of lean, metabolically active muscle mass","a large increase in thyroid hormone secretion during this process"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With aging, lean muscle mass tends to decrease and is partly replaced by less active fat; since muscle is more metabolically active than fat, this shift lowers the basal metabolic rate over the years. — Ch 73, slides 9-11"},{"q":"The relatively large thermic effect of a protein-rich meal reflects the energy cost of...","choices":["digesting fiber that accompanies the protein","processing and metabolizing the amino acids after absorption","storing the protein directly as body fat under most normal conditions","cooling the body after the warm meal is eaten"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The specific dynamic action (thermic effect) of protein is high because the body expends considerable energy deaminating amino acids, forming urea, and processing the products; this can raise metabolic rate substantially more than carbohydrate or fat. — Ch 73, slide 10; Ch 72, slide 6"},{"q":"Air flows into the lungs during inspiration because, by Boyle's law, expanding the thorax...","choices":["compresses the lung to squeeze air inward","lowers alveolar pressure below atmospheric pressure","raises alveolar pressure above atmospheric pressure","has no effect on pressure, so air enters by active pumping"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. As inspiratory muscles enlarge the thoracic cavity, lung volume increases and (by Boyle's law, pressure falling as volume rises) alveolar pressure drops just below atmospheric; air then flows down this pressure gradient into the lungs. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 14-18"},{"q":"Throughout the normal breathing cycle, intrapleural pressure remains...","choices":["higher than alveolar pressure at all times under most conditions throughout the body","positive during quiet expiration to force air out","more negative than alveolar pressure, keeping the lung against the chest wall","exactly equal to atmospheric pressure during inspiration"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Intrapleural pressure stays below (more negative than) alveolar pressure across the cycle; this transpulmonary pressure difference holds the lung expanded against the chest wall and prevents it from collapsing. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 16-19"},{"q":"By the law of Laplace, without surfactant a small alveolus would tend to...","choices":["become completely rigid and unable to change size","generate less collapsing pressure than a large alveolus","collapse and empty into a connected larger alveolus","expand indefinitely at the expense of larger alveoli"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Laplace's law says that for a given surface tension, smaller alveoli generate higher collapsing pressure; without surfactant, a small alveolus would empty into a larger connected one. Surfactant lowers tension more in small alveoli, stabilizing them. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 22-24"},{"q":"Vital capacity is defined as the maximum volume of air that can be...","choices":["expelled after the deepest possible inspiration","moved during a single quiet resting breath","held in the lungs at the end of a normal expiration","left in the lungs after a maximal forced expiration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Vital capacity is the greatest amount of air a person can exhale after taking the deepest possible breath; it equals the sum of tidal volume, inspiratory reserve, and expiratory reserve, and excludes the residual volume that cannot be exhaled. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 27-31"},{"q":"Residual volume cannot be measured by ordinary spirometry because it is the air that...","choices":["can be forcibly inhaled beyond a normal breath","is exhaled with every ordinary quiet breath during this process","remains in the lungs and can never be voluntarily exhaled","moves through the conducting airways each minute"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Residual volume is the air left in the lungs even after a maximal expiration; since it can never be exhaled, spirometry (which measures moved air) cannot capture it, and indirect methods such as gas dilution are required. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 30-31"},{"q":"Surfactant is chiefly composed of...","choices":["cartilage, which stiffens the alveolar wall for support","mucus, which traps inhaled particles in the alveoli","phospholipid, which interposes between water molecules to reduce surface tension","hemoglobin, which binds oxygen within the alveolus under most conditions in the body"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Surfactant is largely phospholipid (notably dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine); its molecules insert among the water molecules lining the alveoli, weakening their cohesive attraction and thereby lowering surface tension. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 22-23"},{"q":"Oxygen moves from alveolar air into pulmonary capillary blood because...","choices":["alveolar oxygen partial pressure is higher than that in the arriving blood","alveolar oxygen pressure is lower than that in the blood","carbon dioxide drags oxygen along with it into the blood","the blood actively pumps oxygen out of the alveolus as it is usually described here"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Gas exchange is driven by partial-pressure gradients: alveolar PO2 (~100 mmHg) exceeds the PO2 of the venous blood entering the pulmonary capillaries (~40 mmHg), so oxygen diffuses down its gradient into the blood until equilibration. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 61-66"},{"q":"According to the principles governing diffusion, the rate of gas transfer across the respiratory membrane increases with...","choices":["greater surface area and a thinner membrane","a smaller partial-pressure gradient across the membrane","lower solubility of the gas in the membrane","a thicker membrane and a smaller surface area"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Diffusion rate rises with a larger surface area, a shorter diffusion distance (thinner membrane), a steeper partial-pressure gradient, and higher gas solubility; the healthy lung maximizes area and minimizes thickness to favor exchange. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 57-62"},{"q":"The anatomic dead space refers to...","choices":["the alveoli that receive air but no blood flow","the residual air that can never be exhaled during this process","the total volume of air moved in one minute","the volume of conducting airways where no gas exchange occurs"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Anatomic dead space is the air filling the conducting zone — nose, trachea, and bronchi — which has no alveoli and therefore participates in no gas exchange; only the air reaching the alveoli contributes to gas exchange. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 32-35"},{"q":"The diaphragm receives its motor innervation from the...","choices":["phrenic nerve, arising from cervical spinal segments","sympathetic chain, acting through spinal ganglia","vagus nerve, which also controls the heart rate","intercostal nerves that supply only the rib muscles"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The diaphragm is driven by the phrenic nerve, which originates from cervical spinal segments (about C3–C5); this is why high cervical spinal cord injury can paralyze the diaphragm and abolish spontaneous breathing. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 12-13"},{"q":"The Hering-Breuer inflation reflex helps protect the lungs by...","choices":["forcing a deeper breath whenever the lungs are stretched","contracting the diaphragm independently of the brainstem","inhibiting further inspiration when stretch receptors sense large inflation","sensing arterial oxygen and driving ventilation during this particular process"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Stretch receptors in the airway walls, when strongly stimulated by large lung inflation, send signals that inhibit the inspiratory center; this reflex helps prevent overinflation of the lungs, mainly during deep breathing. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 89-90"},{"q":"The P50 of the oxygen-hemoglobin curve is the partial pressure at which hemoglobin is...","choices":["fully saturated with the maximum amount of oxygen","50% saturated, serving as a measure of its affinity for oxygen","completely stripped of all of its bound oxygen","binding carbon dioxide rather than oxygen as it typically occurs here"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. P50 is the PO2 at which hemoglobin is half saturated; a higher P50 means lower affinity (the curve is shifted right, favoring unloading), and a lower P50 means higher affinity (left shift), so P50 is a convenient index of oxygen affinity. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 78-81"},{"q":"Fetal hemoglobin has a higher affinity for oxygen than adult hemoglobin, which helps the fetus by...","choices":["releasing oxygen more readily than adult hemoglobin does","carrying carbon dioxide instead of oxygen to the tissues","favoring transfer of oxygen from maternal blood across the placenta","preventing the fetus from taking up any maternal oxygen during this process"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Because fetal hemoglobin binds oxygen more tightly (its curve is left-shifted), it can pull oxygen from the maternal circulation across the placenta even at the relatively low oxygen levels found there, ensuring adequate fetal oxygen supply. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 78-82"},{"q":"Of the total oxygen carried in arterial blood, the overwhelming majority is...","choices":["bound to hemoglobin, with only a small fraction dissolved in plasma","attached to plasma proteins such as albumin during this particular process","carried as bicarbonate within the red blood cells","dissolved in the plasma, with little bound to hemoglobin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. About 98–99% of the oxygen in blood is bound to hemoglobin; only a tiny fraction is physically dissolved in plasma. This is why hemoglobin concentration is such a critical determinant of oxygen delivery. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 76-77"},{"q":"The Haldane effect describes how, in the lungs, oxygenation of hemoglobin...","choices":["promotes the release of carbon dioxide from the blood","converts carbon dioxide directly into oxygen","has no effect on carbon dioxide transport at all","increases the blood's ability to hold carbon dioxide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The Haldane effect is the reciprocal of the Bohr effect: as hemoglobin binds oxygen in the lungs, its ability to hold CO2 (as carbamino compounds and via H+ buffering) falls, promoting CO2 unloading for exhalation. In tissues, deoxygenation enhances CO2 uptake. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 83-87"},{"q":"Carbon dioxide diffuses across membranes more readily than oxygen despite a smaller gradient because CO2...","choices":["is actively transported across the membrane by pumps","is much more soluble, which offsets its smaller partial-pressure difference","binds hemoglobin at the same site oxygen uses as it is usually described here","has a much larger partial-pressure gradient than oxygen"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Although the partial-pressure gradient for CO2 across the respiratory membrane is far smaller than that for oxygen, CO2's roughly 20-fold greater solubility allows it to diffuse about as fast, so CO2 exchange is rarely limiting. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 61-62, 86"},{"q":"The bicarbonate buffer system links respiration to acid-base balance because...","choices":["the buffer works only in the kidney and not in the blood under most conditions in the body","bicarbonate is exhaled directly by the lungs to remove acid","CO2 combines with water to form carbonic acid, which dissociates to H+ and bicarbonate","hemoglobin converts bicarbonate into oxygen for transport"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. CO2 + H2O reversibly forms carbonic acid, which dissociates into H+ and bicarbonate. Because the lungs control CO2, changing ventilation shifts this equilibrium and thus blood pH — the mechanistic link between breathing and acid-base status. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 91-92, 100"},{"q":"Compared with respiratory adjustment, the kidneys correct acid-base disturbances...","choices":["by exhaling excess acid as carbon dioxide gas during this particular process","more slowly, over hours to days, but with greater ultimate capacity","more quickly, within seconds, than the lungs can","without any effect on the blood's bicarbonate level"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The lungs adjust CO2 within minutes for rapid but limited compensation, whereas the kidneys act over hours to days by excreting acid and regulating bicarbonate; the renal response is slower to develop but can more completely restore balance. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 91-92, 100"},{"q":"Central and peripheral chemoreceptors differ in that the central chemoreceptors...","choices":["directly measure the amount of oxygen bound to hemoglobin","lie in the medulla and respond mainly to CO2-driven changes in H+","lie in the carotid bodies and sense mainly low oxygen","sense arterial blood pressure at the aortic arch as a general rule"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Central chemoreceptors sit in the medulla and respond chiefly to H+ generated when CO2 crosses into brain fluid, whereas the peripheral chemoreceptors (carotid and aortic bodies) respond to low arterial oxygen as well as to CO2 and pH. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 91-99"},{"q":"Comparing alveolar gas with the systemic venous blood arriving at the lungs, the alveoli have...","choices":["a higher oxygen and a lower carbon dioxide partial pressure than that blood","identical oxygen and carbon dioxide pressures to that blood","a lower oxygen and a higher carbon dioxide partial pressure than that blood","no oxygen at all, so none can diffuse into the blood"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Alveolar air has higher PO2 and lower PCO2 than the venous blood entering the pulmonary capillaries; these opposing gradients drive oxygen into the blood and carbon dioxide out of it, so blood leaves the lungs oxygenated and CO2-depleted. — Pulmonary I & II, slides 63-66, 71-74"}]};
